  <info>
    <link type="guide" xref="index#plugins"/>
    <title type="link" role="trail">Babble Help</title>
    <title type="text">Plugins</title>
    <title type="link">Plugins</title>
    <desc>Turn plugins on, and what the included ones do.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Plugins</title>

  <p>
    Plugins add to Babble: an entry in a post's menu, a banner on a
    hashtag's page, and settings of their own. Open <gui>Plugins</gui> in
    the main menu to see them. Every plugin starts turned off; the switch
    beside it turns it on, and the button with a gear opens its settings
    when it has any.
  </p>
  <p>
    <gui>Included With Babble</gui> lists the plugins that come with the
    app. <gui>Installed by You</gui> lists the ones you added yourself, by
    putting a plugin's folder in the folder that <gui>Open Plugins
    Folder</gui> opens: <file>~/.local/share/babble/plugins</file>, or
    <file>~/.var/app/io.github.mauriciobc.Babble/data/babble/plugins</file>
    when Babble is installed as a Flatpak.
  </p>
  <note style="warning">
    <title>Only turn on plugins you trust</title>
    <p>
      A plugin runs as part of Babble and can do anything Babble can,
      including reading and posting with your account. The plugins
      included with Babble are reviewed with it; any other plugin is only
      as safe as whoever wrote it.
    </p>
  </note>

  <section id="wallabag">
  <title>Wallabag</title>
  <p>
    Adds <gui>Save to Wallabag</gui> to every post's menu, to keep the
    post's link to read later in <link href="https://wallabag.org">Wallabag</link>.
    The first time, it asks you to sign in: the server's address
    (<sys>app.wallabag.it</sys> for the hosted service, or your own), an
    API client's ID and secret, which you create in Wallabag under
    <gui>API Clients Management</gui>, and your username and password.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your password is used once to sign in and is not stored. The keyring
    keeps the client secret and a token that keeps you signed in; if it
    expires, Babble asks you to sign in again. <gui>Sign Out</gui> is in
    the plugin's settings.
  </p>
  </section>

  <section id="hashpanel">
  <title>Hashpanel</title>
  <p>
    <link href="https://github.com/mauriciobc/hashpanel">Hashpanel</link>
    follows the daily hashtags of the Brazilian fediverse, such as
    <sys>#segundaficha</sys> on Mondays. On the page of a hashtag it
    follows, the plugin shows whose day it is, today's favorites, boosts
    and replies, the week against the one before, and the day's top post.
  </p>
  <p>
    Babble asks Hashpanel only about the hashtags it follows: opening any
    other hashtag sends Hashpanel nothing. Another Hashpanel server can be
    set in the plugin's settings.
  </p>
  </section>

  <section id="plugin-problems">
  <title>When a Plugin Does Not Work</title>
  <p>
    A plugin that cannot be loaded, or that stops working, shows why under
    its name in <gui>Plugins</gui>, with a warning sign; one that stops
    working is also turned off, and Babble says so. The system log has the
    details. A plugin that could not be loaded is tried again the next
    time Babble starts, and one made for another version of Babble is not
    loaded at all.
  </p>
  </section>

</page>
